"Internal Error ()\nciteturn9view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6view0\",\"lineno\":165}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMAX9924–MAX9927-Variable Reluctance Sensor Interfaces with Differential Input and Adaptive Peak Threshold (https://www.analog.com/MAX9924/datasheet)\nciteturn9view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 23; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view0\",\"lineno\":710}); Total lines: 1255\nL583@P8-9: Functional Diagrams (continued)MAX9926\nL584@P9: 100kΩ\nL585@P9: 100kΩ\nL586@P9: 10kΩ\nL587@P9: 100kΩ\nL588@P9: VCC\nL589@P9: VCC\nL590@P9: MODE\nL591@P9: LOGIC\nL592@P9: IN1-\nL593@P9: 100kΩ\nL594@P9: VCC\nL595@P9: IN1+\nL596@P9: BIAS1\nL597@P9: OP AMP\nL598@P9: COMPARATOR\nL599@P9: 30%\nL600@P9: BUFFER\nL601@P9: INTERNAL\nL602@P9: REFERENCE\nL603@P9: 2.5V\nL604@P9: VMIN\nL605@P9: THRESHOLD\nL606@P9: 85ms\nL607@P9: WATCHDOG\nL608@P9: PEAK\nL609@P9: DETECTOR\nL610@P9: EXT1\nL611@P9: COUT1\nL612@P9: ZERO_EN\nL613@P9: GND\nL614@P9: VCC\nL615@P9: 100kΩ\nL616@P9: 100kΩ\nL617@P9: 100kΩ\nL618@P9: VCC\nL619@P9: IN2-\nL620@P9: 100kΩ\nL621@P9: VCC\nL622@P9: IN2+\nL623@P9: BIAS2\nL624@P9: OP AMP\nL625@P9: COMPARATOR\nL626@P9: 30%\nL627@P9: BUFFER\nL628@P9: VMIN\nL629@P9: THRESHOLD\nL630@P9: 85ms\nL631@P9: WATCHDOG\nL632@P9: PEAK\nL633@P9: DETECTOR\nL634@P9: EXT2\nL635@P9: COUT2\nL636@P9: DIRN DIRN\nL637@P9: FLIP-FLOP\nL638@P9: CLK\nL639@P9: INT_THRS1\nL640@P9: INT_THRS2\nL641@P9: www.maximintegrated.com Maxim Integrated │ 10\nL642@P9: MAX9924–MAX9927 Variable Reluctance Sensor Interfaces with\nL643@P9: Differential Input and Adaptive Peak Threshold\nL644@P9-10: Functional Diagrams (continued)MAX9927\nL645@P10: VCC\nL646@P10: IN1-\nL647@P10: VCC\nL648@P10: IN1+\nL649@P10: BIAS1\nL650@P10: OP AMP\nL651@P10: COMPARATOR\nL652@P10: 30%\nL653@P10: BUFFER\nL654@P10: VMIN\nL655@P10: THRESHOLD\nL656@P10: 85ms\nL657@P10: WATCHDOG\nL658@P10: PEAK\nL659@P10: DETECTOR\nL660@P10: COUT1\nL661@P10: EXT1\nL662@P10: GND\nL663@P10: OUT1\nL664@P10: VCC\nL665@P10: VCC\nL666@P10: IN2-\nL667@P10: VCC\nL668@P10: IN2+\nL669@P10: BIAS2\nL670@P10: OP AMP\nL671@P10: COMPARATOR\nL672@P10: 30%\nL673@P10: BUFFER\nL674@P10: VMIN\nL675@P10: THRESHOLD\nL676@P10: 85ms\nL677@P10: WATCHDOG\nL678@P10: PEAK\nL679@P10: DETECTOR\nL680@P10: COUT2\nL681@P10: OUT1\nL682@P10: EXT2\nL683@P10: MODE\nL684@P10: LOGIC\nL685@P10: INT_THRS2\nL686@P10: INT_THRS1\nL687@P10: www.maximintegrated.com Maxim Integrated │ 11\nL688@P10: MAX9924–MAX9927 Variable Reluctance Sensor Interfaces with\nL689@P10: Differential Input and Adaptive Peak Threshold\nL690@P10-11: Functional Diagrams (continued)Detailed Description\nL691@P11: The MAX9924–MAX9927 interface with variable reluc\u0002tance (VR) or magnetic coil sensors. These devices\nL692@P11: produce accurate pulses aligned with flywheel gear-teeth\nL693@P11: even when the pickup signal is small and in the presence\nL694@P11: of large amounts of system noise. They interface with\nL695@P11: new-generation differential VR sensors as well as legacy\nL696@P11: single-ended VR sensors.\nL697@P11: The MAX9924/MAX9925 integrate a precision op amp, a\nL698@P11: precision comparator, an adaptive peak threshold block,\nL699@P11: a zero-crossing detection circuit, and precision matched\nL700@P11: resistors (MAX9924). The MAX9926 and MAX9927 are\nL701@P11: dual versions of the MAX9924 and MAX9925, respec\u0002tively. The MAX9926 also provides a rotational output that\nL702@P11: is useful for quadrature-connected VR sensors used in\nL703@P11: certain high-performance engines.\nL704@P11: The input op amp in the MAX9925/MAX9927 are typically\nL705@P11: configured as a differential amplifier by using four external\nL706@P11: resistors (the MAX9924/MAX9926 integrate precision\u0002matched resistors to give superior CMRR performance).\nL707@P11: This input differential amplifier rejects input common\u0002mode noise and converts the input differential signal from\nL708@P11: a VR sensor into a single-ended signal. The internal com\u0002parator produces output pulses by comparing the output\nL709@P11: of the input differential amplifier with a threshold voltage\nL710@P11: that is set depending on the mode that the device is in\nL711@P11: (see the Mode Selection section).\nL712@P11: Mode Selection\nL713@P11: The MAX9924/MAX9926 provide four modes of opera\u0002tion: Mode A1, Mode A2, Mode B, and Mode C as\nL714@P11: determined by voltages applied to inputs ZERO_EN and\nL715@P11: INT_THRS (see Tables 1, 2, and 3). In Modes A1 and\nL716@P11: A2, the internal adaptive peak threshold and the zero\u0002crossing features are enabled. In Mode A2, an internally\nL717@P11: generated reference voltage is used to bias the differential\nL718@P11: amplifier and all internal circuitry instead of an external\nL719@P11: voltage connected to the BIAS input—this helps reduce\nL720@P11: external components and design variables leading to a\nL721@P11: more robust application. In Mode B, the adaptive peak\nL722@P11: threshold functionality is disabled, but zero-crossing func\u0002tionality is enabled. In this mode, an external threshold\nL723@P11: voltage is applied at EXT allowing application-specific\nL724@P11: adaptive algorithms to be implemented in firmware. In\nL725@P11: Mode C, both the adaptive peak threshold and zero\u0002crossing features are disabled and the device acts as\nL726@P11: a high-performance differential amplifier connected to a\nL727@P11: precision comparator (add external hysteresis to the com\u0002parator for glitch-free operation).\nL728@P11: Table 1. MAX9924/MAX9926 Operating Modes\nL729@P11: Table 2. MAX9925 Operating Modes\nL730@P11: Table 3. MAX9927 Operating Modes\nL731@P11: OPERATING\nL732@P11: MODE\nL733@P11: SETTING DEVICE FUNCTIONALITY\nL734@P11: ZERO_EN INT_THRS ZERO CROSSING ADAPTIVE PEAK\nL735@P11: THRESHOLD\nL736@P11: BIAS VOLTAGE\nL737@P11: SOURCE\nL738@P11: A1 VCC VCC Enabled Enabled External\nL739@P11: A2 GND GND Enabled Enabled Internal Ref\nL740@P11: B VCC GND Enabled Disabled External\nL741@P11: C GND VCC Disabled Disabled External\nL742@P11: OPERATING MODE\nL743@P11: SETTING DEVICE FUNCTIONALITY\nL744@P11: ZERO_EN INT_THRS ZERO CROSSING ADAPTIVE PEAK THRESHOLD\nL745@P11: A1 VCC VCC Enabled Enabled\nL746@P11: B VCC GND Enabled Disabled\nL747@P11: C GND VCC Disabled Disabled\nL748@P11: OPERATING MODE\nL749@P11: SETTING DEVICE FUNCTIONALITY\nL750@P11: INT_THRS ZERO CROSSING ADAPTIVE PEAK THRESHOLD\nL751@P11: A1 VCC Enabled Enabled\nL752@P11: B GND Enabled Disabled\nL753@P11: www.maximintegrated.com Maxim Integrated │ 12\nL754@P11: MAX9924–MAX9927 Variable Reluctance Sensor Interfaces with\nL755@P11-12: Differential Input and Adaptive Peak ThresholdDifferential Amplifier\nL756@P12: The input operational amplifier is a rail-to-rail input and\nL757@P12: output precision amplifier with CMOS input bias currents,\nL758@P12: low offset voltage (VOS) and drift. A novel input archi\u0002tecture eliminates crossover distortion at the operational\nL759@P12: amplifier inputs normally found in rail-to-rail input struc\u0002tures. These features enable reliable small-signal detec\u0002tion for VR sensors.\nL760@P12: The MAX9924/MAX9926 include on-chip precision\u0002matched low-ppm resistors configured as a differential\nL761@P12: amplifier. High-quality matching and layout of these resis\u0002tors produce extremely high DC and AC CMRR that is\nL762@P12: important to maintain noise immunity. The matched ppm\u0002drift of the resistors guarantees performance across the\nL763@P12: entire -40°C to +125°C automotive temperature range.\nL764@P12: Bias Reference\nL765@P12: In Modes A1, B, and C, a well-decoupled external\nL766@P12: resistor-divider generates a VCC/2 signal for the BIAS\nL767@P12: input that is used to reference all internal electronics in the\nL768@P12: device. BIAS should be bypassed with a 0.1μF and 10μF\nL769@P12: capacitor in parallel with the lower half of the resistor\u0002divider forming a lowpass filter to provide a stable external\nL770@P12: BIAS reference.\nL771@P12: The minimum threshold, adaptive peak threshold, zero\u0002crossing threshold signals are all referenced to this volt\u0002age. An input buffer eliminates loading of resistor-dividers\nL772@P12: due to differential amplifier operation. Connect BIAS to\nL773@P12: ground when operating in Mode A2. An internal (2.5V\nL774@P12: typical) reference is used in Mode A2, eliminating external\nL775@P12: components.\nL776@P12: Adaptive Peak Threshold\nL777@P12: Modes A1 and A2 in the MAX9924–MAX9927 use an\nL778@P12: internal adaptive peak threshold voltage to trigger the\nL779@P12: output comparator. This adaptive peak threshold voltage\nL780@P12: scheme provides robust noise immunity to the input VR\nL781@P12: signal, preventing false triggers from occurring due to bro\u0002ken tooth or off-centered gear-tooth wheel. See Figure 1.\nL782@P12: The sensor signal at the output of the differential gain\nL783@P12: stage is used to generate a cycle-by-cycle adaptive\nL784@P12: peak threshold voltage. This threshold voltage is 1/3 of\nL785@P12: the peak of the previous cycle of the input VR signal.\nL786@P12: As the sensor signal peak voltage rises, the adaptive\nL787@P12: peak threshold voltage also increases by the same ratio.\nL788@P12: Conversely, decreasing peak voltage levels of the input\nL789@P12: VR signal causes the adaptive peak threshold voltage\nL790@P12: used to trigger the next cycle also to decrease to a new\nL791@P12: lower level. This threshold voltage then provides an arm\u0002ing level for the zero-crossing circuit of the comparator\nL792@P12: (see the Zero Crossing section).\nL793@P12: If the input signal voltage remains lower than the adaptive\nL794@P12: peak threshold for more than 85ms, an internal watchdog\nL795@P12: timer drops the threshold level to a default minimum\nL796@P12: threshold (VMIN_THRESH). This ensures pulse recogni\u0002tion recovers even in the presence of intermittent sensor\nL797@P12: connection.\nL798@P12: The internal adaptive peak threshold can be disabled and\nL799@P12: directly fed from the EXT input. This mode of operation\nL800@P12: is called Mode B, and allows implementations of custom\nL801@P12: threshold algorithms in firmware. This EXT voltage is typi\u0002cally generated by filtering a PWM-modulated output from\nL802@P12: an onboard microcontroller (μC). An external operational\nL803@P12: amplifier can also be used to construct an active lowpass\nL804@P12: filter to filter the PWM-modulated EXT signal.\nL805@P12: Figure 1. Adaptive Peak Threshold Operation\nL806@P12: 20ms\nL807@P12: V1\nL808@P12: 40ms 60ms\nL809@P12: COUT\nL810@P12: VR\nL811@P12: SIGNAL\nL812@P12: ADAPTIVE\nL813@P12: THRESHOLD\nL814@P12: SET BY V1\nL815@P12: ADAPTIVE\nL816@P12: THRESHOLD\nL817@P12: SET BY V2 MIN\nL818@P12: THRESHOLD\nL819@P12: 80ms 100ms 120ms 140ms 160ms\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMAX9140/MAX9141/MAX9142/MAX9144 - 40ns, Low-Power, 3V/5V, Rail-to-Rail Single-Supply Comparators (https://www.analog.com/media/en/technical-documentation/data-sheets/MAX9140-MAX9144.pdf)\nciteturn9view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 11; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"lineno\":430}); Total lines: 741\nL247@P2-3: Electrical Characteristics (continued)(VCC = 3.0V, VCM = 0V, CL = 15pF, VOD = 10mV, TA = +25°C, unless otherwise noted.)\nL248@P3: 320\nL249@P3: 322\nL250@P3: 326\nL251@P3: 324\nL252@P3: 328\nL253@P3: 330\nL254@P3: -40 -20 0 20 40 60 80 100\nL255@P3: OUTPUT LOW VOLTAGE\nL256@P3: vs. TEMPERATURE\nL257@P3: MAX9140 TOC02\nL258@P3: TEMPERATURE (°C)\nL259@P3: VOL (mV)\nL260@P3: ISINK = 4mA\nL261@P3: 5.00\nL262@P3: 5.05\nL263@P3: 5.10\nL264@P3: 5.15\nL265@P3: 5.20\nL266@P3: -40 -20 0 20 40 60 80 100\nL267@P3: OUTPUT HIGH VOLTAGE\nL268@P3: vs. TEMPERATURE\nL269@P3: MAX