P5: — — 6 6 INB- Comparator B Inverting Input\nL439@P5: — — 7 7 OUTB Comparator B Output\nL440@P5: — — — 8 OUTC Comparator C Output\nL441@P5: — — — 9 INC- Comparator C Inverting Input\nL442@P5: — — — 10 INC+ Comparator C Noninverting Input\nL443@P5: 2 4 4 11 GND Ground\nL444@P5: — — — 12 IND+ Comparator D Noninverting Input\nL445@P5: — — — 13 IND- Comparator D Inverting Input\nL446@P5: — — — 14 OUTD Comparator D Output\nL447@P5: 3 2 — — IN+ Noninverting Input\nL448@P5: 4 3 — — IN- Inverting Input\nL449@P5: — 6 — — SHDN Shutdown: MAX9141 is active when SHDN is driven high;\nL450@P5: MAX9141 is in shutdown when SHDN is driven low.\nL451@P5: — 5 — — LE The output is latched when LE is low. The latch is transparent\nL452@P5: when LE is high.\nL453@P5: 1 7 — — OUT Comparator Output\nL454@P5: — 8 — — N.C. No Connection. Not internally connected.\nL455@P5: OUTPUT\nL456@P5: 2V/div\nL457@P5: INPUT\nL458@P5: 50mV/div\nL459@P5: PROPAGATION DELAY (tPD-)\nL460@P5: MAX9140TOC13\nL461@P5: 10ns/div\nL462@P5: VOD = 10mV\nL463@P5: VCC = 5.5V\nL464@P5: OUTPUT\nL465@P5: 2V/div\nL466@P5: INPUT\nL467@P5: 50mV/div\nL468@P5: PROPAGATION DELAY (tPD+)\nL469@P5: MAX9140TOC14\nL470@P5: 10ns/div\nL471@P5: VCC = 5.5V VOD = 10mV\nL472@P5: OUTPUT\nL473@P5: 2V/div\nL474@P5: INPUT\nL475@P5: 50mV/div\nL476@P5: SINUSOID RESPONSE AT 4MHz\nL477@P5: MAX9140TOC15\nL478@P5: 50ns/div\nL479@P5: VCC = 5.5V\nL480@P5: www.analog.com Analog Devices │ 6\nL481@P5: MAX9140/MAX9141/\nL482@P5: MAX9142/MAX9144\nL483@P5: 40ns, Low-Power, 3V/5V, Rail-to-Rail\nL484@P5: Single-Supply Comparators\nL485@P5: Pin Description\nL486@P5-6: Typical Operating Characteristics (continued)Detailed Description\nL487@P6: The MAX9140/MAX9141/MAX9142/MAX9144 single\u0002supply comparators feature internal hysteresis, high\nL488@P6: speed, and low power. Their outputs are pulled to within\nL489@P6: 300mV of either supply rail without external pullup or\nL490@P6: pulldown circuitry. Rail-to-rail input voltage range and low\u0002voltage single-supply operation make these devices ideal\nL491@P6: for portable equipment. The devices interface directly to\nL492@P6: CMOS and TTL logic.\nL493@P6: Most high-speed comparators oscillate in the linear region\nL494@P6: because of noise or undesired parasitic feedback. This\nL495@P6: tends to occur when the voltage on one input is at or\nL496@P6: equal to the voltage on the other input. To counter the\nL497@P6: parasitic effects and noise, the devices have an internal\nL498@P6: hysteresis of 1.5mV.\nL499@P6: The hysteresis in a comparator creates two trip points:\nL500@P6: one for the rising input voltage and one for the falling input\nL501@P6: voltage (Figure 1). The difference between the trip points\nL502@P6: is the hysteresis. The average of the trip points is the offset\nL503@P6: voltage. When the comparator’s input voltages are equal,\nL504@P6: the hysteresis effectively causes one comparator input\nL505@P6: voltage to move quickly past the other, thus taking the\nL506@P6: input out of the region where oscillation occurs. Standard\nL507@P6: comparators require hysteresis to be added with external\nL508@P6: resistors. The devices’ fixed internal hysteresis eliminates\nL509@P6: these resistors. To increase hysteresis and noise margin\nL510@P6: even more, add positive feedback with two resistors as a\nL511@P6: voltage divider from the output to the noninverting input.\nL512@P6: Figure 1 illustrates the case where IN- is fixed and IN+ is\nL513@P6: varied. If the inputs were reversed, the figure would look\nL514@P6: the same, except the output would be inverted.\nL515@P6: The MAX9141 includes an internal latch that allows\nL516@P6: storage of comparison results. The LE pin has a high input\nL517@P6: impedance. If LE is high, the latch is transparent (i.e., the\nL518@P6: comparator operates as though the latch is not present).\nL519@P6: The comparator’s output state is latched when LEis pulled\nL520@P6: low (Figure 2).\nL521@P6: Shutdown Mode (MAX9141 Only)\nL522@P6: The MAX9141 shuts down when the SHDN pin is low. When\nL523@P6: shut down, the supply current drops to less than 12μA, and\nL524@P6: the three-state output becomes high impedance. The SHDN\nL525@P6: pin has a high-input impedance. Connect SHDN to VCC for\nL526@P6: normal operation. Exit shutdown with LE high (transparent\nL527@P6: state); otherwise, the output will be indeterminate.\nL528@P6: Input Stage Circuitry\nL529@P6: The devices include internal protection circuitry that prevents\nL530@P6: damage to the precision input stage from large differential\nL531@P6: input voltages. This protection circuitry consists of two\nL532@P6: back-toback diodes between IN+ and IN- as well as two\nL533@P6: series 4.1kΩ resistors (Figure 3). The diodes limit the\nL534@P6: differential voltage applied to the internal circuitry of the\nL535@P6: comparators to be no more than 2VF, where VF is the\nL536@P6: forward voltage drop of the diode (about 0.7V at +25°C).\nL537@P6: For a large differential input voltage (exceeding 2VF), this\nL538@P6: protection circuitry increases the input bias current at IN+\nL539@P6: (source) and IN- (sink).\nL540@P6: F (IN+ - IN-) - 2V Input Current 2 4.1k = × Ω\nL541@P6: Input current with large differential input voltages should\nL542@P6: not be confused with input bias current (IB). As long as the\nL543@P6: differential input voltage is less than 2VF, this input current\nL544@P6: is equal to IB. The output is in the correct logic state if one\nL545@P6: or both inputs are within the common-mode range.\nL546@P6: Figure 1. Input and Output Waveform, Noninverting Input\nL547@P6: Varied\nL548@P6: VTRIP+\nL549@P6: VHYST\nL550@P6: VTRIP\u0002COMPARATOR\nL551@P6: OUTPUT\nL552@P6: VOH\nL553@P6: VOL\nL554@P6: VTRIP+ + VTRIP\u00022\nL555@P6: VOS =\nL556@P6: VIN- = 0\nL557@P6: VIN+\nL558@P6: www.analog.com Analog Devices │ 7\nL559@P6: MAX9140/MAX9141/\nL560@P6: MAX9142/MAX9144\nL561@P6: 40ns, Low-Power, 3V/5V, Rail-to-Rail\nL562@P6-7: Single-Supply ComparatorsOutput Stage Circuitry\nL563@P7: The MAX9140/MAX9141/MAX9142/MAX9144 contain a\n"
